HARNESS NOTES (post-run disclosure):
- run 1 crashed on a REAL PRODUCT BUG: the naive orderBy [{verifiedAt: desc}] put UNVERIFIED rows first because Postgres sorts NULLS FIRST on DESC — the exact inverse of the "verified first" contract. Fixed by switching the unfiltered list to a two-pool read with manual pagination (verified pool verifiedAt desc, then unverified pool updatedAt desc); the fix is exactly what run 3's asserts rank-first + verified=true(count) prove.
- run 2 crashed on harness hygiene, not product: run 1 had left a VERIFIED VERIFY-ASSOC row behind (crashed after creating it, before cleanup), and run 2 took its seeded snapshot WITHOUT a prefix preflight — so verified=true found 2 rows. Fixed by moving the VERIFY-prefix + verify-assoc-email wipe into a proper preflight that proves zeros before snapping the baseline.
- run 3 fully green with CLEANUP COMPLETE + per-row byte-identical restore check of all 6 seeded rows (the console shows mangled accents for the same-name/description lines: that is the documented codepage-850 rendering artifact, NOT file corruption — the byte-level jest compare passed, and the mojibake regex check was done during the commit step).
- the seed file (prisma/seed-associations.ts) is contact-hollow by design: no invented emails/phones/social handles; the team curates contacts and stamps verifiedAt from curation.
- pagination across the two pools was asserted indirectly (default page covers all 6 + 3 VERIFY rows era). An off-by-one across a page boundary was NOT separately driven (52+ rows would be needed); the slicing logic is unit-read simple (offset/take per pool) but is a known untested edge.